  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "S.Guruprasad Kamath vs Food Inspector on 14 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Thursday, The 14Th Day Of January 2016/24Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 2859 of 2009 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 267/2006 Of The Additional Sessions Court (Adhoc-Ii), Kasaragod Cc 38/2001 Of The Judicial First Class Magistrate Court, Kasaragod Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Accused: -------------------------------------- S.Guruprasad Kamath, S/O.S.Mohan Kamath, Kamath Cream Parlour Oberla Complex, Kumbla, Koipady Village Kasaragod Taluk. By Adv. Sri.T.G.Rajendran Respondents/Complainant And State: --------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Kasaragod Circle. 2. State Of Kerala Rep:By Public Rosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. R By Public Prosecutor Sri.V.S.Sreejith This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 14-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. S.Guruprasad Kamath vs Food Inspector on 14 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/130411835/ 1 B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P. No.2859 of 2009 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 14th day of January, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 5.10.2000 at about 2.00 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 900 grams of ice cream from the shop of the accused. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. The Public Analyst, after analysis submitted Ext.P4 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for ice cream under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P9 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would also show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for ice cream under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW3 were examined and Exts. P1 to P21 were marked for the prosecution. No evidence was adduced on the side of the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P9 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P9 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in S.Guruprasad Kamath vs Food Inspector on 14 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/130411835/ 2 Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P9 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Therefore, Ext. P9 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in S.Guruprasad Kamath vs Food Inspector on 14 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/130411835/ 2 Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
